  "body": "Found by the read-only second review of PR #2892. It is the defect of #2878 in a second post. The finding comes from a read of the code and of the golden. No run on a control proves it.\n\n## What happens\n\nWith the post option \"Threading: G76 cycle\", the Tormach lathe post (`assets/posts/tormach-lathe.cps:826-852`) writes the LinuxCNC `G76` cycle. The control moves the tool from the start point to the depth of each pass at rapid traverse, at the start Z. The start Z is inside the length of the part.\n\n- The golden `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/tormach-lathe/thread_g76_mm.nc` shows `G0 Z14` and then `G76` on a bar that continues past Z14.\n- PR #2892 repairs the Fanuc lathe post only. Its test `lathe_post_tormach_thread.rs:282-285` sets `start_in_air = true` by hand. That shows that the Tormach start is not in free air.\n\nA rapid move of the threading insert into solid material is a collision that passes. Thus the priority is `P1`.\n\n## What is expected\n\nThe law of #2878 applies to each post that writes a thread cycle: the cycle starts in free air (in front of the end face, or in a relief groove wider than the insert), or the post is refused by name before it writes a file.\n\n## Done when\n\n- The Tormach lathe post refuses a `G76` cycle whose start point stands above material, with the check of PR #2892 (`g76_start`).\n- The golden shows a start in free air.\n- Each other post with a thread cycle is checked, and the result is listed here.\n\nRefs #2878\n",
